    summary: "Yttrium-90, used for radioembolisation (TARE/SIRT) and older radioimmunotherapy agents, is a pure beta emitter with no directly imageable gamma or positron emission, which makes patient-specific pre-therapy dosimetry difficult. Yttrium-86 solves that problem: it is chemically identical to yttrium-90, so an 86Y-labelled version of the same antibody, peptide or microsphere construct has an identical biodistribution, and its 14.7-hour half-life with about 33% positron emission allows PET imaging over two to three days, long enough to build a full time-activity curve and calculate the organ and tumour doses a subsequent 90Y therapy would deliver.\n\nIt is produced on a cyclotron via 86Sr(p,n)86Y on an enriched strontium-86 target (typically SrCO3 or SrO), the preferred route because it gives the highest radionuclidic purity; the main radionuclidic impurity, 87mY, is normally kept below a few percent when highly enriched target material is used. Bombardments of a few hours at modest beam currents (2-6 microamps) are typical.\n\nThe approach was first used clinically at Forschungszentrum Jülich for pre-therapy dosimetry ahead of 90Y-citrate treatment of bone metastases, and has since been applied to 86Y-DOTA-peptide imaging ahead of 90Y-labelled somatostatin analogue therapy; it remains a research and specialist-dosimetry tool rather than a routinely stocked clinical isotope, since both the cyclotron target and the imaging protocol are non-standard.",
